          ]
        }
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.6,
      "why": "Full pipelining gives 1 result/cycle, so at any Fmax >= 32 MHz it clears the 32 MS/s throughput constraint and the 0.44 us p99-batch window (32 results take 32 cycles; at 32 MHz that is exactly 1.0 us of compute, but the batch deadline is measured from tick to last result and the L1 bound assumes the unit is free at the tick, so the binding term is latency + 31/Fmax; keeping N modest keeps latency_ns small). Accuracy needs ~12.3 bits, which is reachable with W in 14..20 and N in 12..20; the wide W/N ranges let Optuna trade area (luts_plus_ffs) against accuracy_bits to map the front. angle_guard -1..2 and frac_guard 0..2 cover the small guard-bit trade-offs that shift area without changing the accuracy floor much."
    },
    {
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "param": "data_width",
          "low": 14,
          "high": 20
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "n_iter",
          "low": 12,
          "high": 20
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "angle_guard",
          "low": -1,
          "high": 2
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "frac_guard",
          "low": 0,
          "high": 2
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "rounding",
          "low": null,
          "high": null
        ,
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ]
        },
        {
          "param": "m",
          "low": 2,
          "high": 8
        ,
          "choices": null
        }
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.4,
      "why": "pipelined_m registers every m stages, cutting FF count (and often LUTs from shorter combinational chains) at the cost of higher latency (ceil(N/m)+2). Since the objective is min luts_plus_ffs and the batch deadline (0.44 us) plus 32 MS/s throughput are the hard constraints, m is the key knob that trades area against latency/Fmax. Sweeping m=2..8 across the same W/N/guard ranges lets the search find the cheapest m that still meets the p99-batch and throughput bounds, and exposes the area-vs-latency trade-off on the front. Same W/N/guard ranges as pipelined so the two families' fronts are directly comparable and merge cleanly."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "No fleet facts yet, so this is a cold-start round. The spec's hard constraints are: (1) throughput >= 32 MS/s, (2) max_abs_err <= 2^-12 (~12.3 accuracy bits), (3) sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.44. Only pipelined and pipelined_m are available to me, and both give 1 result/cycle, so throughput is met whenever Fmax >= 32 MHz, which is comfortably reachable for these widths. The accuracy floor (~12.3 bits) is the binding accuracy constraint: it rules out very small W or very small N, so I set data_width 14..20 and n_iter 12..20, which brackets the region where accuracy_bits crosses 12.3 while leaving room on both sides so the front is not clipped at the constraint boundary. angle_guard -1..2 and frac_guard 0..2 cover the small guard-bit trade-offs that affect area and accuracy margin. For pipelined (budget 0.6) the front is driven by W/N/guard; for pipelined_m (budget 0.4) the extra m=2..8 knob is the main area-reduction lever, so I give it a meaningful share to map the area-vs-latency trade-off. The 0.44 us p99-batch deadline is the tightest system constraint: 32 results at 1/cycle need 32 cycles of compute plus pipeline latency, so at the minimum Fmax that satisfies throughput (32 MHz) the compute alone is 1.0 us, which exceeds 0.44 us. This means the design must run at a higher Fmax (roughly >= 32/0.44 ~ 73 MHz for the compute term to fit, before latency) or the L1 bound must show the batch fits; keeping N modest (12..20) and m small-to-moderate keeps latency_ns low so the Fmax required to meet 0.44 us stays achievable. The split 0.6/0.4 favours the simpler pipelined family for the bulk of the front while still sampling pipelined_m's area savings. Rounding is included as a 2-choice parameter in both families since it shifts accuracy by up to ~0.5 LSB and area slightly, and the search should not be forced to pick one."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "The front is well-established with 6 feasible designs spanning a wide trade-off curve (luts_plus_ffs 1318..2696, accuracy_bits 14.1..15.7). The best area design (pipelined_m, 1318 luts_plus_ffs) comfortably meets all constraints: throughput 93.6 MSPS >= 32, max_abs_err 5.84e-05 <= 0.000244, and sys_p99_batch_us is well within 0.44 us (best seen 0.165). The hypervolume of 8414 indicates good coverage of the objective space. Since this is the final round, the exploration is complete."
  ,
  "next_plan": null
}
